mode: "100644"
text: "# Batch days, setup recipes & camera-shy on-ramps\n\n## The batch day (a month of talking heads in one afternoon)\nPull 4–8 scripts from `short-form-video-script` (slots from batch-content-plan / content-calendar), then:\n1. **Prep (night before):** print/queue each script as a beat map (3–5 beats + verbatim first/last lines); stack\n   3–4 tops by the tripod; charge the phone, clear storage (4K eats it).\n2. **Setup (2 min):** eye-level phone, face the window, mic check with a 10s test clip — *listen to it on\n   headphones* before rolling anything.\n3. **Order:** hardest/highest-stakes script first; the fun one last as a reward.\n4. **Per script:** say the hook first and fresh · retake per beat, keep rolling · three-strike rule on stubborn\n   lines · cap ~10 min per short script.\n5. **Between scripts:** swap top, 60s off-camera, water, reset to +20% energy; log the keeper take number.\n6. **Stop at ~60–90 min** even mid-list — a dead-eyed take costs more than an empty slot.\n7. **Hand off:** footage → captions-and-clipping / capcut / descript; publish via scheduling-and-queue →\n   **WoopSocial**; read results natively / via analytics-and-reporting.\n\n## Setup recipes (phone-first, no gear snobbery)\n| Recipe | Setup | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Desk** (default) | Phone on tripod at eye level, window in front or beside, wired lav or quiet room | Overhead lights carving eye shadows; window behind you |\n| **Walking** | Front camera handheld at arm's length, slightly above eye level, outdoors | Wind (cup the mic side, or wireless lav with a windscreen); traffic noise; don't read while walking |\n| **Car** (parked) | Phone on a dash/vent mount, daylight through the windscreen | Engine off (audio hum); it reads casual — match the content to that register |\n| **Standing/whiteboard** | Phone further back, landscape or 9:16 with headroom, lav essential at distance | Energy must go up with distance; check on-screen text stays in safe zones |\n\nUpgrade path (only when a named limit is hit): wired lav → wireless lav (moving shots) → soft key light (rooms\nwith no usable window). That's the whole list; a new camera is last, not first.\n\n## The camera-shy user (a valid requirement, not a flaw to fix)\nNever shame someone onto camera. Offer three honest routes and let them choose:\n- **Avatar route:** a disclosed synthetic presenter via `heygen` (creator/social lane) or `synthesia`\n  (enterprise/L&D/localization lane). Honest trade-off: avatars scale but read polished-clinical — trust-led\n  founder content genuinely works better with a real face, which is why those skills route it here. The hybrid\n  is the pro move: film the short trust layer yourself, let the avatar carry the informational volume.\n- **Faceless route:** screen-records, B-roll, photos + a voice track — their real voice, or `ai-voiceover`\n  (disclosed). Plenty of large channels are faceless; it's a format, not a consolation prize.\n- **The on-ramp** (for \"I want to, but…\"): voice-only first → hands/desk/over-the-shoulder shots → face on\n  camera for a single 15s piece delivered to one imagined friend. Small, reversible steps; no deadline.\n\n## The perfectionist (the other failure mode)\nSymptoms: 30 takes, \"just one more,\" a folder of unpublished footage. Prescription: **cap takes per beat at 3**,\npick the take where they sound like themselves (not the smoothest one), and ship it. Take quality peaks by ~2–3;\nafter that you're trading presence for polish, and the audience only pays for presence. A published good-enough\ntake gathers data; a perfect draft gathers dust.\n\n## Worked example A — \"Blunt indie founder\" (desk recipe, batch of 5)\nSunday, 75 min: phone on books at eye level, window left, wired lav. Five 30s scripts as beat maps; hooks\ndelivered first-take-fresh; per-beat retakes with claps; top swapped each script; stopped after script 5 with\nenergy intact. Monday: footage to capcut, queue via scheduling-and-queue → WoopSocial. One afternoon, two weeks\nof posts, zero new gear.\n\n## Worked example B — \"Camera-shy bookkeeping studio\" (hybrid)\nOwner won't film weekly explainers — fine. Route the 12-part FAQ series to synthesia (disclosed stock avatar,\nlocked scripts). Keep ONE real piece to camera: a 20s \"why I started this\" filmed on the on-ramp (voice memo →\ndesk shot → face), used on the profile and in social-proof-and-testimonials content. Trust layer human,\nvolume layer avatar, nobody shamed.\n\n## Never\nJudge a take you can't see, or fabricate \"that looked natural\" · prescribe a camera upgrade as the fix · shame a\ncamera-shy human onto camera · let a word-for-word prompter read ship when a beat-map riff was available · film\nanyone else without consent · claim WoopSocial films, edits, or analyzes footage (publishing only) · sand a\ntake past the point where the human sounds like themselves.\n"
